### Multi-segment SD-WAN via Cloud Backbone
- **Presenter:** Linda Dunbar
- **Slide Deck:** [03-multi-segment-sdwan](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rtgwg-03-multi-segment-sdwan-00)
- **Draft:** `draft-ietf-rtgwg-multisegment-sdwan`
- **Discussion:**
  - Linda Dunbar presented updates addressing IESG review comments. Key changes include resolving Geneve encapsulation conflicts with HMAC authentication (removing TTL fields from sub-TLVs when HMAC is used), standardizing terminology around permitted and excluded regions, and clarifying soft versus hard exclusion behaviors.
  - Additional updates include specifying outer protocol types (EtherType, next header 50 for ESP payload), handling unknown sub-TLVs using the length field, moving HMAC SHA-256 sub-TLV definitions to Section 4, requiring ingress gateways to regenerate outer headers with their own IP source address, adding an MTU applicability section, and explicitly moving billing and key establishment out of scope.
  - Yingzhen Qu suggested requesting another directorate review to verify holistic document integration before proceeding to WGLC; Linda Dunbar agreed.

### A Simple BGP-based Mobile Routing System for the Aeronautical Telecommunications Network
- **Presenter:** Fred Templin
- **Slide Deck:** [02-BGP-based Mobile Routing System for the Aeronautical Telecommunications Network](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rtgwg-02-bgp-based-mobile-routing-system-for-the-aeronautical-telecommunications-network-00)
- **Draft:** `draft-ietf-rtgwg-atn-bgp`
- **Discussion:**
  - Fred Templin outlined updates in draft version -35, including secure dynamic DNS updates for scalable mapping, prefix delegation per RFC 9663, updates for RPKI (RFC 6480) and BGP ASN security, and a new section on PKI and DNS security.
  - Dependencies remain on 6MAN drafts (`aero-omni-amendments`, AERO, OMNI) which act as normative references.
  - John Scudder (in chat) noted that the WG can progress the document even with normative downrefs, but publication as an RFC will wait until the cluster progresses. Yingzhen Qu and Jeff Tantsura discussed whether changing references to informational would prevent publication delays if 6MAN drafts remain paused. Susan Hares (in chat) requested a BGPDIR review.

### Problem Statement for Moving Some Information Out of the IGP
- **Presenter:** Joel Halpern
- **Slide Deck:** [04-Problem Statement for Moving Some Information Out of the IGP](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rtgwg-04-problem-statement-for-moving-some-information-out-of-the-igp-00)
- **Discussion:**
  - Joel Halpern presented a problem statement proposing an offload mechanism and standardized interfaces for long-term stable, non-forwarding information (e.g., node capabilities, VPN membership) to prevent overloading IGPs. Inter-operator information exchange is explicitly out of scope.
  - AC Lindem and Les Ginsburg noted that previous efforts (such as IS-IS and OSPF separate distribution channels/application IDs) exist but saw zero adoption, questioning whether this problem warrants prioritization or new work outside LSR/IDR.
  - Joel Halpern responded that existing IGP distribution mechanisms do not match operator needs when IGPs reject non-routing data, necessitating an alternative offload tool.
  - Jeff Haas noted that operators often put state into IGPs because of the topological context (attaching to nodes/links) and suggested leveraging BGP-LS transport infrastructure.
  - Tony Lee argued for architectural separation, stating that routing protocols should not become general distributed databases and that non-routing data should be offloaded to external servers.

### Link Overload Alleviation Proposals (Consolidated Discussion)
The working group reviewed several related drafts addressing link congestion and overload alleviation. Chairs repeatedly requested authors to consolidate these overlapping proposals.

1. **Elastic-Bandwidth-Routing-Framework**
   - **Presenter:** Wu Chang Cheong
   - **Slide Deck:** [05-Elastic-Bandwidth-Routing-Framework](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rtgwg-05-elastic-bandwidth-routing-framework-00)
   - **Discussion:** Wu Chang Cheong presented an approach using existing IGP TE metrics combined with SRv6 to dynamically distribute traffic across primary and secondary paths without requiring new IGP protocol extensions. Field trial results from China Mobile were shared. Jeff Tantsura and Tony Lee pointed out that this approach closely resembles existing Tactical Traffic Engineering (TTE) mechanisms and questioned the need for a separate document.

2. **Common Approaches to Link Overload Alleviation**
   - **Presenter:** Yisong Liu
   - **Slide Deck:** [06-Common Approaches to Link Overload Alleviation](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rtgwg-06-common-approaches-to-link-overload-alleviation-01)
   - **Discussion:** Yisong Liu summarized six functional actions for link overload alleviation (configuration, measurement, capacity-aware database, triggering, notification, traffic steering) and categorized existing solutions (distributed mitigation, EBR, DTE/TTE, RCM). The document is informational. Les Ginsburg noted an upcoming contribution on telemetry-driven path optimization (TSPO). In chat, Vishnu Beeram, Andrew Stone, and Joel Halpern discussed terminology around overload definitions and suggested structuring comparison tables mapping actions to solutions.

3. **BGP-based Inter-domain Elastic Bandwidth-aware Routing Framework (BBEAR)**
   - **Presenter:** Ke Zhu
   - **Slide Deck:** [07-BGP-based Inter-domain Elastic Bandwidth-aware Routing Framework](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rtgwg-07-bgp-based-inter-domain-elastic-bandwidth-aware-routing-framework-00)
   - **Discussion:** Ke Zhu presented BBEAR to alleviate inter-domain link congestion using inbound BGP attribute adjustments (MED/AS-Path) and outbound SR steering. Jeff Tantsura raised strong concerns about flooding dynamic bandwidth updates over BGP, noting that 30-second update intervals provide stale data that can cause instability, and advised taking BGP modifications to IDR.

4. **Congestion Avoidance Using Segment Routing**
   - **Presenter:** Michelle Mi
   - **Slide Deck:** [08-Congestion Avoidance Using Segment Routing](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rtgwg-08-congestion-avoidance-using-segment-routing-00)
   - **Discussion:** Michelle Mi presented a local congestion avoidance mechanism utilizing TI-LFA / LFA backup paths and SR SIDs when link utilization thresholds are breached. Peter Psenak cautioned that using LFA/TI-LFA mechanisms for congestion management across multiple links can lead to routing loops, as LFA is strictly designed for single-link failure scenarios.

### Routing Mechanism in Dragonfly Networks
- **Presenter:** Yingya Guo
- **Slide Deck:** [09-Routing Mechanism in Dragonfly Networks](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rtgwg-09-routing-mechanism-in-dragonfly-networks-00)
- **Discussion:**
  - Yingya Guo presented an informational gap analysis and requirement set for Dragonfly, Dragonfly+, and Dragonfly Ultra topologies used in HPC and AI clusters. Key gaps include load imbalance between minimal and non-minimal links and the lack of standardized cross-vendor congestion notifications.
  - Jeff Tantsura noted that fast notifications belong within the scope of the FAN working group effort, while control plane topology handling can be built using existing BGP multi-VRF mechanisms. Tony Lee pointed out existing TE mechanisms for load information exchange.

### HPC Scheduler Job Metadata & Service Intent YANG Models
- **Presenter:** Guangying Zheng
- **Slide Deck:** [10-hpc-scheduller-job-metadata-service-intent](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rtgwg-10-hpc-scheduller-job-metadata-service-intent-00)
- **Discussion:**
  - Guangying Zheng presented proposed YANG data models for HPC/AI scheduler job metadata and service intent to allow job schedulers to communicate network requirements to network controllers.
  - Jeff Tantsura and Kireeti Kompella discussed scope. Jeff Tantsura questioned the direct relevance to routing protocols, suggesting the authors demonstrate how this interacts with existing job schedulers (e.g., Slurm) and provisions network routing parameters. Kireeti Kompella agreed to clarify the connection to routing.

### SRv6-Based Trusted Key Relay in Quantum Key Distribution (QKD) Networks
- **Presenter:** Min Zhang
- **Slide Deck:** [11-A Framework for SRv6-Based Trusted Key Relay in Quantum Key Distribution Networks](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rtgwg-11-a-framework-for-srv6-based-trusted-key-relay-in-quantum-key-distribution-networks-00)
- **Discussion:**
  - Min Zhang introduced a framework using SRv6 paths to perform dynamic, multi-hop trusted key relay across QKD networks, taking into account link quantum key generation rates and node key relay capabilities.
  - Yingzhen Qu noted that the draft contains several incomplete sections (TBDs) and encouraged further refinement and discussion on the mailing list.

### Scenarios and Routing Requirements for Mega-Constellation LEO Satellite Networks
- **Presenter:** Xinyao Wang
- **Slide Deck:** [12-Scenarios and Routing Requirements for Mega-Constellation LEO Satellite Networks](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rtgwg-12-scenarios-and-routing-requirements-for-mega-constellation-leo-satellite-networks-00)
- **Discussion:**
  - Xinyao Wang presented three deployment scenarios (multi-vendor intra-constellation, terrestrial network transport, and satellite payload systems) and seven routing requirements for LEO satellite networks facing fast topology changes and limited onboard computing power.
  - Greg Mirsky asked for clarification on single vs. multi-operator domains and questioned computing constraints if space data centers are deployed. Xinyao Wang clarified the focus is single-operator multi-vendor networks with strict thermal, power, and weight constraints.
  - Tony Lee asked for specific gaps relative to RFC 9717. Xinyao Wang explained that the goal is maintaining reachability and path continuity without requiring globally synchronized SPF re-computations.

### Adaptive Routing Framework
- **Presenter:** Yingya Guo
- **Slide Deck:** [13-Adaptive Routing Framework](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rtgwg-13-adaptive-routing-framework-00)
- **Discussion:**
